    "contents": [
      "A Text with content: publication and citation data by region for AI journal \r\narticles, conference papers, repositories, and patents.",
      "A Text with content: Figure 1.1.1 shows the number of AI publications in \r\nthe world. From 2010 to 2021, the total number of \r\nAI publications more than doubled, growing from \r\n200,000 in 2010 to almost 500,000 in 2021.",
      "A Text with content: The fgures below capture the total number \r\nof English-language and Chinese-language AI \r\npublications globally from 2010 to 2021\u2014by type, \r\nafliation, cross-country collaboration, and cross\u0002industry collaboration. The section also breaks down ",
      "A Text with content: This section draws on data from the Center for Security and Emerging Technology (CSET) at Georgetown University. CSET maintains a \r\nmerged corpus of scholarly literature that includes Digital Science\u2019s Dimensions, Clarivate\u2019s Web of Science, Microsoft Academic Graph, \r\nChina National Knowledge Infrastructure, arXiv, and Papers With Code. In that corpus, CSET applied a classifer to identify English\u0002language publications related to the development or application of AI and ML since 2010. For this year\u2019s report, CSET also used select \r\nChinese AI keywords to identify Chinese-language AI papers; CSET did not deploy this method for previous iterations of the AI Index report.1",
      "A PageFooter with content: 24",
      "A SectionHeader with content: 1.1 Publications",
      "A SectionHeader with content: Total Number of AI Publications",
      "A Text with content: In last year\u2019s edition of the report, publication trends were reported up to the year 2021. However, given that there is a signifcant lag in the \r\ncollection of publication metadata, and that in some cases it takes until the middle of any given year to fully capture the previous year\u2019s \r\npublications, in this year\u2019s report, the AI Index team elected to examine publication trends only through 2021, which we, along with CSET, \r\nare confdent yields a more fully representative report.",
      "A figure with content unknown",
      "A SectionHeader with content: Overview",
      "A figure with content unknown",
      "A Text with content: 1 See the Appendix for more information on CSET\u2019s methodology. For more on the challenge of defning AI and correctly capturing relevant bibliometric data, see the AI Index team\u2019s \r\ndiscussion in the paper \u201cMeasurement in AI Policy: Opportunities and Challenges.\u201d"
    ]
  },
